# failed in ways a document can be wrong about but a command cannot: 75 files
# left uncommitted, this hook never once run, and a note filed where the other
#
# ⚠ THIS LANE IS DELIBERATELY LOOSE, and that is a design decision, not
# second commit of every afternoon and be disabled inside a week — and this
# hook already has one comment in its history that lied about a red lane and
# taught people to ignore an exit code. So it fires only when the note is
# genuinely behind: more than 10 commits, or more than 2 days old.
#
# ⚠ AND BE PLAIN ABOUT WHAT IT CANNOT CATCH, WHICH IS THE FAILURE IT WAS BUILT
# FOR. This is a PRE-COMMIT lane. It runs when somebody commits, and only then.
# — files on the floor, this hook never run, no note written — and no
# pre-commit lane can fire on that, because nothing was committed for it to run
# in front of.
#   IT CATCHES  a note falling behind a repository people are still committing
#               to: >10 commits, or >2 days, since the newest entry.
#   IT CANNOT   a session that ends with nothing committed; an entry whose prose
#               is thin or wrong; a claim never released; this hook never run.
# before you stop. Nothing here can force it, and a lane that tried would be a
# lane people delete. The age is measured against the LATER of today and HEAD's
# date, because at pre-commit time HEAD is the previous commit — measuring only
# against it hid exactly the staleness that accrues while nobody commits.
# The claim machinery itself, which has to be incapable of blocking: four shapes
# of unreadable claim file (no `paths`, `paths` not a list, not JSON, and a
# directory named *.json), a real clash, a stale one, and the newest-entry rule.
# A claim file with no `paths` key once threw a TypeError out of --check, which
# is the LAST line of this hook, which made its exit status this hook's exit
# status — the warning that promises never to refuse anything refused a commit.
# And the other half: is somebody else in the files you are committing?
# The claim listing is VISIBILITY, NOT LOCKING — no `|| exit 1` here on purpose.
# It prints nothing at all unless a claim held by another agent covers a path
# you changed, and it exits 0 even then. An agent that is told will usually
# behave; an agent that is blocked deletes the block.

# ⚠ AND THE LAST LINE'S STATUS IS THIS SCRIPT'S STATUS. A shell script exits
# with the status of its last command, so the un-gated warning above was one
# unhandled exception away from refusing a commit — and it did exactly that,
# once, on a claim file with no `paths` key. --check now guarantees exit 0
# line is the fourth, and it costs nothing. Every lane that is ALLOWED to refuse
# a commit says so with its own `|| exit 1` above.
exit 0
